const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Proofreading","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/proofreading/","description":"Academic and scientific proofreading at $0.025 a word, inside the EFA published band. Precision proofreading for researchers and academics — English.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between proofreading and editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The difference is the stage, not the effort. Proofreaders “check for typographical errors and formatting mistakes” by comparing “the latest stage of the project to the previous one”. Copyediting “involves correcting spelling, grammar, usage, and punctuation, checking cross-references, and preparing the style sheets that guide consistency and accuracy”.\n\nThe decisive phrase is in the first: proofreading is defined against a previous stage. It assumes somebody has already edited the document. On our sample we marked the same 400 words both ways — fourteen marks as a proofread, thirty-nine as a copyedit, $62 apart across a 6,200-word manuscript."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know which one I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ask what has already happened to the document, not what you would like done to it.\n\nProfessionally edited, or typeset? Proofreading. Read by a colleague? Probably a copyedit — a colleague reads for whether the argument holds, which is a different activity. Revised only by you? Almost certainly a copyedit: nobody reliably sees their own usage errors or the sentence they rewrote twice and left half-rewritten. Never edited at all? Not this service.\n\nThe fastest way to settle it is the free comparison: send 1,000 words and you get the same passage marked both ways, with the counts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of errors do you correct?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Five service areas, and the largest is the one nobody expects.\n\nTypographical errors and misspellings, including proper nouns and drug names that spellcheckers pass. Punctuation, mostly around clauses that moved during revision. Revision artefacts — half-deleted phrases, doubled words across line breaks, sentences left without a full stop. On our sample this was the biggest category, 61 of 247, because a document revised four times has been proofread none. Consistency of terms, abbreviations, hyphenation, units and spelling convention. And the apparatus: caption numbering, cross-references, declarations, and the abstract read against the body."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you check my references?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two checks, and it is worth knowing which you are buying.\n\nThe consistency check is included: every citation has an entry, every entry is cited, years match, format is uniform. On our sample, 18 discrepancies in 54 references — all introduced during revision, because reference managers do not reconcile what an author types by hand into a tracked-changes file.\n\nThe accuracy check is separate, at $90: verifying each reference exists as described and that DOIs resolve. Neither check verifies that a source says what you claim it says — that is scholarship, and it is yours."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"English is not my first language. Is proofreading enough?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually not, and we would rather say so before you pay. Article use, preposition selection, idiom and the calqued constructions that are correct English and obviously written by somebody thinking in another language all sit above the typographical level. A proofreader will leave every one of them.\n\nWe recommend line editing at $0.050 a word or a copyedit at $0.035, and we will show you the difference on 1,000 words of your own text. The work should move toward your voice at its clearest rather than a house style — particularly for a thesis, which you will defend aloud. You also get a pattern note: your five commonest constructions, so the next paper needs less of this."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you proofread my thesis, and will my university allow it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is the safest thing to buy when a policy is unclear. The IPEd guidelines for editing theses, July 2025 restrict professional thesis editing to “copyediting and proofreading” and say editors “should not make corrections to the content, substance or structure of the thesis”. Proofreading sits squarely inside what is permitted.\n\nTwo things still apply. Your own institution’s regulation overrides every external guideline and will be in the graduate handbook rather than on the public site. And IPEd asks for written supervisor approval and for the editor to be named in your acknowledgements — we ask for the first and supply wording for the second. The bounded service is on our thesis editing page."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/proofreading/"}]}];

